# =============================================================================
# .dockerignore -- what NOT to copy into the image
# =============================================================================
# When you run `docker build .`, Docker first sends the whole folder to its
# engine. This file excludes things from that copy.
#
# WHY IT MATTERS (three real reasons, not just tidiness):
#
#  1. SPEED. Without this, the 500MB .venv folder gets sent on every build.
#  2. CORRECTNESS. .venv holds Windows executables. Copying them into a Linux
#     container gives you a broken environment that fails in confusing ways.
#     The image must install its OWN Linux libraries -- which the Dockerfile does.
#  3. SECURITY. credentials.yml and .env hold secrets. Anyone who pulls your
#     image can read every file inside it. Secrets must be injected at RUN time
#     as environment variables, never baked in at BUILD time.
#
# Copy this file into house-price/ alongside the Dockerfile.
# =============================================================================

# --- Virtual environments (reason 1 and 2 above) ---
.venv/
venv/
env/

# --- Python leftovers ---
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ipynb_checkpoints/

# --- SECRETS: never let these into an image ---
conf/local/credentials.yml
.env
*.pem
*.key
credentials.yml

# --- Data and trained models ---
# WHY EXCLUDE THE MODEL? Two schools of thought:
#   (a) Bake the model in  -> image is self-contained, but you rebuild the whole
#       image for every retrain, and images get large.
#   (b) Load at startup from Blob Storage -> image stays small, model updates
#       without a rebuild. This is what most teams do, and where we are heading.
# We exclude it here and let POST /pipeline/run create one, or load from Azure.
data/
!data/01_raw/.gitkeep
!data/01_raw/

# --- Development-only files ---
.git/
.gitignore
notebooks/
*.ipynb
.viz/
.telemetry
README.md
TUTORIAL.md

# --- Editor and OS noise ---
.vscode/
.idea/
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
